<p>Bangladesh is India's largest onion destination by a wide margin — verified WITS/UN Comtrade data puts it at USD 209.5 million in 2023, roughly a third of India's total onion export value worldwide, well ahead of the next-largest markets (Malaysia at USD 89.3 million, the UAE at USD 81.5 million). That scale reflects pure proximity economics: short transit and minimal freight cost make Indian onion structurally the cheapest reliable option for Bangladeshi buyers. This guide is for importers setting up or re-pricing a Bangladesh onion programme: how to specify grade and packing, what each side files, how India's export policy feeds into your price, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<h2>Which size grade and packing should a Bangladeshi importer order?</h2>
<p>Specify a size grade explicitly — 40-60mm, 50-70mm or 60-80mm — since preferences differ by buyer segment even within Bangladesh. Packing is mesh or net bags (5-25kg) for airflow; properly cured onion in ventilated packaging is what survives the trip in good condition, and sealed formats are not something we'd recommend regardless of what packaging a buyer's retail programme might otherwise prefer.</p>
<p>The trade data shows which segment Bangladesh sits in. Dividing WITS/UN Comtrade 2023 value by tonnage, Bangladesh averaged about USD 237 per tonne, against about USD 293 for Malaysia and USD 261 for Sri Lanka. Bangladesh buys large volumes for wholesale markets, where 40-60mm and mixed medium grades sell. Uniform 60-80mm onion for supermarket packs costs more and suits a smaller part of the Bangladeshi trade. Order the grade your channel sells, not the largest one offered.</p>

<!-- insight:seasonality -->
<h2>When is Indian onion most available, and when is supply tight?</h2>
<p>Supply follows three crops. Rabi onion, harvested from about March and stored in ventilated sheds, made up 70-75% of India's total onion production, according to a March 2025 government release, and it carries the market until kharif onion arrives from October or November. A late kharif crop follows in the new year. Nashik district, with the Lasalgaon and Pimpalgaon APMC mandis as the benchmark markets, supplies most export-grade red onion.</p>
<p>The tight window is roughly September to November, when stored rabi stock is running down and kharif onion has not yet arrived in volume. The October 2023 MEP was introduced because "the quantity of stored rabi 2023 onion is declining", in the government's own words. The rabi crop size is the number to watch: the government estimated 227 lakh tonnes for 2024-25, over 18% above the previous year's 192 lakh tonnes, and prices fell and the export duty was withdrawn soon after.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Dhaka wholesaler supplying wholesale markets year-round books its largest volumes for April to August, from fresh, well-cured rabi stock, and keeps autumn orders small. That is the period when India is most likely to restrict exports and when stored onion is oldest and most likely to sprout.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much onion fits in a container, and why does freight matter so much?</h2>
<p>About 26-28 MT in a 20ft, and the same in a 40ft, because onion reaches the container's weight limit before it fills the space. A 40ft therefore only adds freight cost. Book 20ft containers.</p>
<p>Onion is a low-value cargo. At Bangladesh's 2023 average of about USD 237 per tonne, a 27 MT container carried roughly USD 6,400 of onion. Every USD 640 of freight, handling or border cost is another 10% on the landed price. That is why Bangladesh buys so much Indian onion: no other origin can deliver to Chattogram in 2-5 days or across Petrapole-Benapole in 1-3 days. It is also why a few days' delay at the border, or a container rejected for rot, wipes out the margin on that load.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>Payment usually runs on a letter of credit opened through a Bangladeshi bank under Bangladesh Bank's foreign-exchange rules, so the LC needs to be in place before loading. The export-policy line in the checklist is the one that changes most: whatever MEP, duty or licensing condition is in force on the day of shipment is what the paperwork reflects. On the Bangladesh side, BSTI clearance involves mandatory testing on arrival, separate from the phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin we provide, and the Plant Quarantine Wing import permit applies because this is plant-origin fresh produce — build both into your first-shipment timeline. See our <a href="/markets/bangladesh/">Bangladesh market guide</a> for the full documentation and payment-norms picture.</p>

