<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Onion is volatile even by fresh-produce standards, and we don't publish a flat rate because the market genuinely doesn't move on one. Four things matter most: the size and timing of the Nashik/Maharashtra rabi harvest (the crop that dominates export volume and stores well — a weak or delayed harvest tightens supply fast), India's own domestic price-stabilisation interventions (a Minimum Export Price sets a legal floor, an export duty adds a direct cost, and — as 2023-24 showed — outright export bans are a real historical risk, not a hypothetical one), the size grade you specify (larger grades generally command a premium), and which route you use (sea to Chattogram vs. overland via Petrapole-Benapole have different cost structures, see the comparison below).</p>
<p>We'd rather explain the policy side clearly than have you discover it mid-negotiation. India actively manages onion exports to protect domestic supply and consumer prices — using a minimum export price, at times an export duty, and, from December 2023 to May 2024, an outright export ban of almost five months. These measures have changed with limited notice more than once, and reports suggest the export duty was removed again in April 2025 — which is itself exactly the kind of change that can happen with little warning. So we don't quote onion business as though policy conditions were fixed: when you place an order, we confirm the applicable terms at that point — MEP, any duty, licensing requirements — and your contract reflects what's actually in force. If you're building a recurring Bangladesh onion programme, plan for that variability explicitly rather than assuming a stable baseline.</p>
