<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common quality dispute on onion shipments is sprouting or rot from inadequate curing before packing — onion that goes into mesh bags without a properly dried outer skin and firm neck will degrade in transit regardless of how good the container ventilation is. We cure fully before packing specifically to avoid this, and it's a fair question to ask any supplier: how long was this cured, and to what moisture condition, before bagging?</p>
<p>The second common issue is size-grade mix inconsistency — a shipment nominally graded "50-70mm" that actually contains a wide scatter of sizes creates real problems for a buyer running mechanical sorting or a specific retail-pack format. Specify your tolerance explicitly in the purchase order, not just a target range.</p>
<p>Third, and specific to this lane: when India's export policy shifts mid-order — a new MEP, a duty change, or in the extreme case a ban — the practical reality is that the order has to be requoted or, in the worst case, paused until conditions clear. We tell buyers immediately when we see a policy signal rather than waiting for it to become official, since a few days' lead time can matter for your own downstream planning. Build a policy-contingency clause into any long-term supply agreement rather than assuming price and availability are guaranteed months out.</p>
<p>What first-time Bangladesh buyers most often get wrong: defaulting to sea freight without checking whether the overland route would actually suit their sourcing region and volume better. Petrapole-Benapole can be meaningfully faster and cheaper for western-India-sourced onion, but it depends on border congestion at the time and carries its own documentation pattern separate from sea freight — worth asking about explicitly rather than assuming sea is the default correct choice.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Khulna importer receives a 27 MT load of 50-70mm onion in October. Old rabi stock, bagged before full curing, has started sprouting in the mesh bags. The buyer has to re-sort and loses weight on every bag. The clause that protects against it: sprouting and rot together at no more than an agreed limit within the 2-5% defect band, checked at loading, with photos of cut samples, and a pre-shipment inspection by a named agency on a first order.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
